# Subsection (b)(1) - "Targeted Low-Income Child" Definition

According to 42 U.S. Code § 1397jj(b)(1), a targeted low-income child means a child who:

**(A)** has been determined eligible by the State for child health assistance under the State plan;

**(B)** is either a low-income child OR a child whose family income exceeds the medicaid applicable income level but does not exceed 50 percentage points above it (or meets alternative income criteria);

**(C)** The critical eligibility requirement states: "who is not found to be eligible for medical assistance under subchapter XIX or, subject to paragraph (5), covered under a group health plan or under health insurance coverage (as such terms are defined in section 300gg–91 of this title)."

This provision means the child must lack coverage through Medicaid or private employer-based health insurance to qualify as a targeted low-income child eligible for the program (with limited exceptions for dental-only supplemental coverage under paragraph 5).

